{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":240602,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"27d59094-efa5-44c7-96c3-c0f57b8ec66d","total_cost_usd":1.2533295,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69040,"cache_read_input_tokens":20117,"output_tokens":20062,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69040,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":20062,"cache_read_input_tokens":20117,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69040,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69040},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":51206,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.051311,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":20062,"cacheReadInputTokens":20117,"cacheCreationInputTokens":69040,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.2020184999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"The catalog and most of the params hold up under arithmetic, but two rules fail against the simulator: no band in the shipping slice is actually reachable at base fuel, and the flown ablation curve makes a single plunge the cheapest descent from every band and every load, which is the opposite of what GDD 2.3.1 requires.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_consistent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params is defined in the baseline's bands, and no undefined band is referenced.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Baseline defines exactly {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog uses only those three across all 30 debris entries and all 3 band_summary entries. Params reference them in ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_skims and economy.band_value_multiplier — three keys each, no fourth key, no missing key. Set difference is empty in both directions.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_catalog\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Each band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises on piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"All nine fields recomputed from the array. Suborbital: 9 pieces; masses 110+260+55+400+75+70+360+140+850 = 2320, /9 = 257.78 (claimed 257.78); fragile weight 8+5 = 13 over total weight 105 = 0.12381 (claimed 0.1238). Low: 10 pieces; 230+300+560+200+120+380+700+480+110+1500 = 4580, /10 = 458.00 (claimed 458); fragile 8+5+5 = 18 over 93 = 0.19355 (claimed 0.1935). High: 11 pieces; 700+520+420+360+120+800+630+185+265+670+2400 = 7070, /11 = 642.727 (claimed 642.73); fragile 6+4+2+2+4 = 18 over 73 = 0.24658 (claimed 0.2466). Every field matches to the quoted precision.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Mean piece mass rises band over band, so valuable junk physically fights the player.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"257.78 -> 458.00 -> 642.73 kg, a strict climb of 1.78x then 1.40x, 2.49x end to end. Excluding the crane-only oversized pieces the hand-tetherable means are 1470/8 = 183.75, 3080/9 = 342.22 and 4670/10 = 467.00 kg — 1.86x then 1.36x, also strictly rising, so the climb is not an artefact of the three oversized entries.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted fragile share 13/105 = 0.1238, 18/93 = 0.1935, 18/73 = 0.2466. Strictly rising. Against the GDD's 0.10 and 0.25 endpoints the suborbital band is +23.8% and the high band is -1.4%, both inside any reasonable reading of 'roughly'. The gradient is a real climb rather than two bands sitting on each other: low sits 0.053 below high, 27% of the way down the interval.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted mass per crushed-equivalent slot over hand-tetherable shipping-slice pieces, recomputed from the catalog with fragile pieces charged their uncrushed slot cost: suborbital sum(mass*weight) 17120 over sum(slots*weight) 160 = 107.00 kg/slot; low 32090/168 = 191.01; combined 49210/328 = 150.03. At cargo.base_slots 6 that is 900.18 kg of cargo against flight.dry_mass_kg 900 — a total mass ratio of 2.0002x, i.e. 0.02% from exact doubling and far inside the 25% tolerance. The simulator independently reports kg_per_slot 150.03, full_hold_kg 900.2, full_hold_mass_ratio 2.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"ablation_optimum_is_one_to_two_skims\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The cheapest descent is 1 to 2 skims from the high band, no band's optimum exceeds the high band's, each minimum equals its optimal_skims entry, and the curve is what the stated model produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Three of the four sub-conditions hold on the array. Minima: suborbital [12.86, 13.38, 15.50, 19.30] at index 0; low [21.42, 20.05, 21.14, 24.75] at index 1; high [35.12, 30.72, 30.17, 33.46] at index 2 — high is in range, no optimum exceeds it, and optimal_skims {0, 1, 2} matches exactly. The array also reproduces its own coefficients: toll series 1.42*1.25^j cumulates to 1.42 / 3.195 / 5.414 / 8.187, each skim adds 2e-5*40^3 = 1.28, and entry cost is 2e-5*(heat_index*skim_heat_multiplier[k])^3. Recomputed: high k=0 = 2e-5*119^3 + 1.42 = 33.703+1.42 = 35.12; high k=2 = 2e-5*103.53^3 + 2.56 + 5.414 = 22.194+7.974 = 30.17; low k=1 = 2e-5*92^3 + 1.28 + 3.195 = 15.574+4.475 = 20.05; suborbital k=3 = 2e-5*71.38^3 + 3.84 + 8.187 = 7.274+12.027 = 19.30. All four within 0.01% of the array. The check fails on the fourth condition read against the flown game rather than the spreadsheet: the simulator reports cheapest_pass_count 1 in all nine band x load descents, with ablation_pct rising monotonically in every series without exception — suborbital full hold 18.5/22.4/24.3/25.9, low full hold 15.8/21.6/23.8/25.5, high full hold 16.3/23.2/25.4/26.8. The second pass costs 3.9 to 6.9 plate-percent and returns nothing. The array is internally faithful and still describes a descent the game does not fly, and GDD 2.3.1's 'never a single plunge' is false at every band and every load.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"The entry-heat term is priced off skim_heat_multiplier, which the simulator measures as roughly a fifth of its claimed cooling — adopt the measured arrays and re-derive cycle_toll_base_pct, cycle_toll_growth and heat_cost_coefficient against them, or escalate to the human under 4.5 risk 1, since no toll setting produces a U-curve at the measured cooling.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_multiplier_matches_measured\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches what was flown, entry by entry for the high band, within 0.10 at every index.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Claimed [1.0, 0.92, 0.87, 0.86] against measured high-band [1.0, 0.984, 0.968, 0.954] at fixed entry depth 0. Absolute gaps: 0.000, 0.064, 0.098, 0.094. The largest is 0.098 at index 2, under the 0.10 threshold by 0.002, so the check passes on its stated tolerance and I am not moving that tolerance. It passes narrowly and for a misleading reason: in relative terms the first high-band skim removes 1.6% of entry heat against the claimed 8%, a factor of 5, and the low band is 2.3% against 8%. The absolute gaps stay small only because every measured multiplier sits near 1.0 — i.e. because skimming barely cools anything. See observations; this is the root cause behind the ablation failure above.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_curve_shape_and_toll_growth\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and flattens by its last two entries; cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"skim_heat_multiplier [1.0, 0.92, 0.87, 0.86]: first entry exactly 1.0; successive deltas -0.08, -0.05, -0.01, all non-positive; the final delta is 0.01, a fifth of the previous one and an eighth of the first, so the benefit visibly saturates rather than continuing to fall. cycle_toll_growth 1.25 > 1, which makes the fourth cycle's toll 1.42*1.25^3 = 2.77 plate-percent against a marginal heat saving of only 2e-5*(119*0.87)^3 - 2e-5*(119*0.86)^3 = 22.194 - 21.437 = 0.76 — so indefinite skimming is priced out by a growing toll rather than a flat one.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_descent_speed_full_hold\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Parachute descent speed at full hold is under the 5 m/s soft-landing threshold but above about 3.5 m/s, so the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.70 against landing.soft_landing_ms 5 — 6.0% of margin, inside the band 3.5 to 5. The simulator measures touchdown 4.70 full hold, 4.07 half, 3.32 empty in all nine descents, and those track sqrt(m) on 1800/1350/900 kg to within 0.01 m/s (4.70*sqrt(1350/1800) = 4.070, 4.70*sqrt(900/1800) = 3.323). The upgrade is load-bearing: storage tier 1 (9 slots, 1350 kg cargo, 2250 kg total) gives 4.70*sqrt(2250/1800) = 5.25 m/s, a hard landing, which parachute tier 1 pulls back to 5.25*sqrt(1.8/2.6) = 4.37 m/s.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_at_fifty_percent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50%, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"tow_fee.max_fee_fraction is exactly 0.5, curve is 'linear', free_radius_m 1200. Evaluating fee = 0.5*(d-1200)/(628319-1200): d = 0 gives -0.00096 before clamping and 0 after, d = 1200 gives exactly 0, d = 314759 (midpoint) gives 0.2500, d = 628319 gives exactly 0.5000. 628319 m is half the circumference of the baseline's 200 km planet (pi*200000 = 628318.5), so the cap lands at the antipode as 2.3.3 requires. The fee is a deduction from haul value only, so payout runs 100% down to 50% and never touches savings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap suborbital pieces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"A cheap suborbital piece is size_class_base_value.small 40 times band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 40. Three of them are 120; economy.launch_cost is 100, i.e. 2.50 cheap pieces, inside the GDD's stated 2-3. Full accounting of a lazy run: 100 launch plus a suborbital direct entry at replate_cost_per_pct 2 — 12.86% by the params' own curve gives 25.72, and the simulator's measured 18.5% at full hold gives 37.00 — so worst case 137.00 against four cheap smalls at 160, clearing 23. A full six-slot hold of the worst junk in the sky pays 240 and clears 103.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing tension is below magnet_hold_force_n with margin at the heaviest legal load, so shear never fires during normal flying.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Astronaut acceleration a = jetpack_thrust_n / (suit_mass_kg + total tethered mass), and per-cable tension is m_i * a. base_tether_slots is 2, so the heaviest legal load is two momentum_wheel_cluster at 800 kg: a = 400/(110+1600) = 0.2339 m/s2, tension = 800*0.2339 = 187.1 N against magnet_hold_force_n 900 — a 4.81x margin. Heaviest legal two-piece load inside the shipping slice is two docking_collar at 700 kg: a = 400/1510 = 0.2649, tension = 185.4 N, 4.85x margin. The true worst case is one heavy piece alone, where the denominator is smallest: 400*800/910 = 351.6 N, still 2.56x under the hold force. Tension is bounded above by jetpack_thrust_n 400 N for any load by construction, so 900 N can never be reached under steady thrust at any tether count.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter anywhere reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Enumerated every thrust-bearing field in the params: flight.thrust_n 22000 and flight.rcs_thrust_n 900 both belong to the stage that staging jettisons, and eva.jetpack_thrust_n 400 belongs to the astronaut, which 2.3.1 explicitly sanctions as the get-out-and-push rescue (400 N against a 1800 kg loaded ship is 0.22 m/s2, a rescue nudge and not an engine). There is no post-stage thrust, relight, restart, reserve or residual field in flight, reentry, ablation or landing, and no upgrade in the twelve-entry list has an effect string touching thrust after stage separation.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_and_premium_sufficient\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces are never crushable at any compactor tier present in the params, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier is 1, which crushes small only; there is no field in the params that grants a tier the ability to crush a fragile piece, and the catalog's size_classes block carries no fragile-crush exception. economy.fragile_value_premium is 3, so a fragile medium in the low band is worth 95*2.4*3 = 684 against 228 for a solid medium from the same band — exactly three solid pieces, which satisfies 'several'. In the high band a fragile medium is 95*5.5*3 = 1567.50 against 522.50. The premium also survives the piece's disadvantages: it pays 4 uncrushed slots against 2, so its value per slot is 171 against 114, a 1.5x edge — enough to be a decision rather than a default.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet — hand_tetherable is false for that class.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"catalog.size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable is false; small and medium are both true. Slot costs are 6 crushed / 12 uncrushed for oversized against 1/2 and 2/4, so the class is distinct on both axes. The three oversized entries — scorched_fairing_half 850 kg, ruptured_pressure_vessel 1500 kg, upper_stage_skirt 2400 kg — are one per band and each is the heaviest piece in its band, so the crane-only rule never leaves a band without a heavy.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve upgrade purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 costing more than tier 1, and each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries, 6 distinct parts (fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet), 2 tiers each. Tier 2 > tier 1 on cost in all six: 1100>400, 1250>450, 1000>380, 1400>520, 950>350, 900>300. Each tier improves its named stat monotonically from the base value in the params: fuel_capacity_kg 1200->1600->2100, thrust_n 22000->28000->35000, base_slots 6->9->12, heat_capacity 100->130->165, parachute_drag_coefficient 1.8->2.6->3.6, magnet_hold_force_n 900->1400->2000. No effect string names a field absent from the params.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_bands_reachable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band the shipping slice needs — suborbital and low — is reachable, and the catalog populates both with at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The catalog half passes. Suborbital carries two fragile types (cracked_viewport_glass small, dish_reflector_shard medium) and all three size classes; low carries three fragile types (cracked_solar_array medium, star_tracker_head small, antenna_feed_horn small) and all three size classes. A player who never leaves the slice meets crushing, meets the uncrushable exception, and meets both hand-tetherable classes. The reachability half fails outright: the simulator reports reached=false for all three ascents, 'out of fuel before circularising' — suborbital stalls with 290.17 kg unspent (24.2% margin), low with 210.80 kg (17.6%), high with 153.37 kg (12.8%). The balance_notes assert the base ship makes band one with ~180 m/s to spare (2200*ln(2400/1200) = 1525 against ~1300 of ascent); flown, it makes no band at all. Neither shipping-slice band is attainable at fuel_capacity_kg 1200, so every rule downstream of getting there is untested in practice.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise flight.fuel_capacity_kg (or flight.thrust_n, to cut gravity losses on the ascent) until the suborbital band circularises with margin at base config, and re-check that fuel_tank tier 1 stays a wanted purchase rather than a prerequisite.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"nothing_satisfied_by_moving_the_target\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes only because a quantity the design document fixes in prose was moved.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Checked every number the GDD states as prose against the params. cargo.base_slots 6 matches 2.2's 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' and the appendix's open question; eva.base_tether_slots 2 matches 2.3.4 and the same appendix line; landing.soft_landing_ms 5 matches 2.3.2; landing.no_gear_multiplier 2 and landing.fragile_multiplier 2 match 'double damage'; reentry.heat_capacity 100 matches 'cargo damage begins at 100% heat'; reentry.cargo_damage_interval_s 3 matches 'one cargo slot every ~3 seconds'; reentry.heat_dissipation_s 5 matches 'dissipates in ~5 seconds'; eva.magnet_range_screens 0.125 matches '~1/8 of the screen'; cargo.compactor_tier 1 matches the shipped tier in 4.1; tow_fee.max_fee_fraction 0.5 matches 'capping at 50%'; ablation.plate_capacity_pct 100 matches a full plate. In particular the full_hold_doubles_mass pass above was earned by moving the catalog's piece masses to 150.03 kg per slot, with base_slots 6 and dry_mass_kg 900 both untouched — the arrow moved, not the target.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The skim-multiplier check passes by 0.002 and that is the thinnest result in this audit. High-band measured cooling is [1, 0.984, 0.968, 0.954] against a claimed [1, 0.92, 0.87, 0.86]: absolute gaps of 0.064/0.098/0.094 all sit under the 0.10 tolerance, but in the terms the economy actually uses, the first high-band skim removes 1.6% of entry heat rather than 8% — a factor of five — while still being charged a flat 1.28 plate-percent plus its cycle toll. The check is written on absolute difference and the multipliers all cluster near 1.0, so a passing gap and a fivefold mispricing look identical here. If this array is ever re-tolerated, tighten it on ratio rather than difference.\",\n    \"Skimming cools least exactly where the design needs it most. Measured first-skim cooling runs 0.951 suborbital, 0.977 low, 0.984 high — the band with the hottest entry gets the weakest relief, which is the reverse of the gradient 2.3.1 is built on and the reason the high band's optimum cannot be pulled off index 0 by any toll setting.\",\n    \"Difficulty inverts on the descent the player will actually fly. At the deepest commit the curve is steeper than the params claim (peak heat 100/131.5/163.1 against heat_index 83/100/119), but at the cheapest commit a full-hold return costs 18.5% of plate from suborbital, 15.8% from low and 16.3% from high — the top of the sky is the cheapest ride home. This is 4.5 risk 2 landing, and it is invisible to any check that reads the params alone.\",\n    \"landing.parachute_area_m2 does not exist as a field. descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.7 is asserted in the params and the 603.6 m2 canopy was back-solved from it, by the Balancer and then again by the simulator — so the agreement between them is arithmetic, not evidence. The sqrt(mass) scaling is real and the Storage-versus-Parachute pairing stands; the absolute 4.7 does not until the area is a field the Coder can read.\",\n    \"unstaged_heat_multiplier 3.5 was validated at the wrong mass. The sweep reports unstaged_kg_m2 734.7, which is 900/(0.35*3.5) — the dry mass, not the baseline's 2400 kg fuelled stack at 1959 kg/m2. The unstaged passes that cleared (suborbital peak 73, low peak 83, against the 100 cargo-damage line) were flown 2.7x too light; a slipperier stack penetrates deeper before it decelerates, so the real margin is thinner than 27 and 17 points suggest.\",\n    \"The economy prices junk by size class and band only, with no per-piece value field, so within a class the lightest piece is strictly dominant: insulation_blanket_wad at 55 kg and crumpled_hull_panel at 110 kg are both worth 40 and both cost one crushed slot. The catalog's own design note wants the wad to be 'the right answer when the jetpack is dry'; under flat class pricing it is the right answer always, and the suborbital band's 2x mass spread stops being a decision. Same pattern in the low band between avionics_crate (200) and reaction_wheel_housing (230). Fixing it needs either a per-piece value field on the Balancer's side or a narrower in-class mass spread on the Designer's — neither agent can do it alone.\",\n    \"Two docking_collar on tether is 1400 kg against a 110 kg suit: acceleration 0.265 m/s2, 13.7x sluggier than an empty astronaut, and a 50 m round trip with a stop consumes roughly 55 of the 80 seconds of full-thrust jetpack that eva.jetpack_fuel_kg 24 at 0.3 kg/s buys. It is legal, it never shears, and it is at the outer edge of controllable rather than inside it. Worth flying before it is signed off.\",\n    \"Measured descent wall clock runs 1.06 h for a direct suborbital entry to 4.31 h at the shallowest high-band commit. No value in this file can fix that; time compression is a feature decision, and every candidate fix to the ablation curve makes the problem worse by restoring the multi-pass descents that cost the hours. As currently tuned the game rewards the player for skipping the mechanic that costs the time, which is the only reason the numbers look playable.\",\n    \"The Playtester reports best_score 7 of 8 across 2916 configurations with nothing scoring 8, and traces the conflict to ballistic coefficient: worlds that get real skim cooling cannot survive an unstaged pass, and worlds that survive unstaged get no cooling. If that holds, the ablation failure above is not a tuning bug and should not be sent round the loop twice — it is a design decision for the human, and 4.5's own fallback (flat per-pass ablation with a hard cap on passes) is already written down for it.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":128180,"ttft_stream_ms":2537,"time_to_request_ms":208,"type":"result","duration_ms":238393,"uuid":"d2107da1-e5e6-443c-a4b0-7896af793293"}
